## Deserialization entry points

DESERIALIZATION Z1–Z2. Every call of a cataloged deserialization entry in the input,
with a "may run" call-graph edge to every callback the format may run on a class of
the input it may create there:
- **Java serialization** (`ObjectInputStream#readObject`/`readUnshared`,
  `ObjectInput#readObject`): concrete serializable classes — private non-static
  `readObject(ObjectInputStream)`/`readObjectNoData()` of each class of the serializable
  chain, `readResolve()` with serialization's access rules, an Externalizable class's
  public no-arg constructor and `readExternal`, and the no-arg constructor of the first
  non-serializable superclass;
- **XStream** `fromXML`: any concrete class — `readResolve()` declared in it or a
  superclass, the private `readObject` of each serializable class of its chain, an
  Externalizable class's constructor and `readExternal` (no other constructor: XStream
  allocates without one).

The stream decides which classes appear — a cast after reading does not bound it — so
the edges say *may run*; whether the data is attacker-controlled is not modeled.
**Policy** (Z2): `evaluated` — a policy read from literals in the entry's own method,
on the same object, decides each class (a literal
`setObjectInputFilter(ObjectInputFilter.Config.createFilter("…"))` with JEP 290 class
patterns; XStream's `addPermission(NoTypePermission.NONE|AnyTypePermission.ANY)`,
`allowTypes`/`denyTypes` with literal arrays, `allowTypesByWildcard`/`denyTypesByWildcard`,
`allowTypeHierarchy`, `setupDefaultSecurity`, the last added permission deciding first);
rejected classes get no edge. `unrestricted` — XStream before 1.4.7 (no security
framework), or 1.4.7–1.4.17 with nothing configured. `default_allowlist` — XStream 1.4.18+
with nothing added: no class of the input is admitted. `unknown` — a restriction exists
that is not evaluated (a `resolveClass` override, a non-literal filter, an instance
configured elsewhere or passed to other code): every edge is kept. `none_seen` — nothing
visible; a process-wide `jdk.serialFilter` is not visible in bytecode. Callbacks of JDK
classes (a `HashMap` hashing its keys while being read) and XStream's own converters for
JDK types (the classic gadget chains) are not modeled, so every entry is also an
unbounded model gap.
