## Limitations

- **Reflection, dynamic proxies (AOP/CGLIB, Spring Data repositories, JDK `Proxy`),
  and `invokedynamic` sites other than lambdas/method references** are invisible to a
  static call graph. A Spring Data repository interface, for instance, has no bytecode
  implementation at all — Spring generates it at runtime — so a call to one can only be
  seen as reaching the interface method itself, never the generated proxy's body.
- **Serialization gadget chains and config-driven behavior** (XML wiring, `web.xml`)
  are not modeled beyond a five-chain catalog (see "Candidate gadget chains") — Java serialization and XStream entries reach the callbacks of the
  input's own classes (see "Deserialization entry points"), not the JDK's own callbacks,
  XStream's converters for JDK types, nor what those call on the objects read; only
  literal filters and XStream permissions in the entry's own method are evaluated; a REACHABLE/FRAMEWORK/NOT_REACHABLE verdict only reflects direct and
  virtual/interface (CHA-expanded) method calls found in bytecode. **ServiceLoader**
  provider *declarations* are inventoried (see the section above, SERVICE_LOADING S0),
  and reachable `Iterator.next()` or `Provider.get()` calls can add conditional
  provider-constructor edges when a bounded same-method receiver chain links them to
  the exact load site. The linear `stream().findFirst().get().get()` path is supported
  at `Provider.get()`; a stream without a terminal operation remains lazy. Unlinked
  receiver flows and unsupported stream callback/terminal flows produce incomplete-model warnings. A registration alone is not
  a reachability verdict. JPMS: provider sets follow `-jpms-scenario` (see "JPMS module
  descriptors"); full module resolution and layers other than `ModuleLayer.boot()` are
  not modeled, and no exact dispatch edge follows an object created by a `provider()`
  method.
- **Shaded/relocated dependencies**: an embedded copy is recognized only when something
  names it — a `pom.properties` in the jar, the jar's own maven-shade `artifactSet`
  includes, a hash-tied SBOM, or `-embedded-components` — and its classes confirm it;
  a relocation is applied only when explicit (the jar's maven-shade configuration or
  `-embedded-components`). Unknown relocations are not discovered, and a copy whose
  version cannot be fixed by its classes (up to 8 published versions compared) stays a
  coverage gap.
- **CHA over-approximates**: a virtual/interface call is treated as reaching every
  subtype that declares the same-named method, whether or not that specific override is
  ever actually invoked at runtime for that call site. This produces false positives,
  never false negatives, for the method-dispatch part of reachability.
- **Reflection under-approximates**: anything invoked purely via `java.lang.reflect`,
  `MethodHandle` lookups outside a recognized `LambdaMetafactory` call, or a
  dependency-injection framework wiring beans by name/type at runtime, is invisible to
  this analysis and can produce false negatives (a real path exists that ariadne cannot
  see).
{{if .LLMModel}}- **LLM-assisted mappings (`llm` in the Source column) are unverified, machine-suggested
  method lists.** Each method was checked to exist in the analyzed bytecode, but not that
  it is the vulnerable one. When the model saw only the advisory text (`llm (advisory)`),
  a NOT_REACHABLE verdict is reported as UNKNOWN instead, since that is too weak to call
  safe.
{{end}}{{if .WarningMessages}}
